Add an OHOS/AArch64 system-dependent port to SACLIB.

SACLIB keeps everything that is not portable C in sysdep/.  The shipped ports
cover x86/x86_64 Linux, macOS and SPARC Solaris; aarch64 Linux and OpenHarmony
are not covered at all:
  * mksysdep.pl cannot classify the processor (`uname -mp` reports aarch64) nor
    the OS (`uname -s` reports HarmonyOS), so `bin/sconf` silently installs no
    system-dependent files and the build fails on the missing GC();
  * the x86_64 Linux port defines _X86_LINUX_, which makes hfloats.h include the
    glibc-only <ieee754.h>.  On OpenHarmony the C library is musl-based and has
    no such header, and the enum-style ieee_rep bitfield layout it stands for is
    not the AArch64 one anyway;
  * that port's GC() spills 16 dummy integers to force the registers onto the
    stack, which is the System V AMD64 calling convention (16 argument
    registers in play).  AArch64/AAPCS64 has 8 argument registers plus x8-x18,
    so the same construction has to be re-derived.

The new port therefore uses its own sysdep.h (little endian, 64-bit, no
ieee754.h) and an AAPCS64 GC().  Two further fix-ups in bin/ make the generated
makefile usable on a toolchain that is not the GNU/Linux one:

  * bin/mkmake hard-codes `AFLAGS = -I${saclib}/include -P`, i.e. the GNU `as -P`
    (pre-process) option the old Linux ports relied on for their .s files.
    AArch64 `as` has no -P; mklib now passes an AFLAG the same way it already
    passes SACFLAG, and the recipe sets it to -ansi.  (SACLIB 2.2.8's src/ has
    no .s files left, and the Debian package drops the option as well.)
  * bin/mklib passed only CC to the generated makefile, which hard-codes
    `AR = ar` and `RANLIB = ranlib`.  The OHOS clang toolchain ships ar and
    llvm-ar but no plain ranlib, so the archiver and its indexer have to be
    overridable too — AR and RANLIB are now forwarded from the environment.

Measured on device (HarmonyOS, clang 15, shipped test_package unchanged):
a GC() that only spills argument registers passed 6/6 at -O0 and failed 18/18
at -O1/-O2/-Os, while the setjmp() version below passed 24/24 over the same
matrix.  Removing GC() from the test made every build pass, which is what
isolated the collector.
